  "id": "COMPOUND:42509 - PROTEIN:799",
  "verbalization": "Linderene acetate is implicated in the activation of Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1 through a multi-step pathway involving UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-10. This enzyme is a target of multiple compounds, including (2E,4E)-N-Isobutyl-6-(2-thienyl)hexa-2,4-dienamide and 5-Methoxy-2,2,10-trimethyl-3,4,4a,5-tetrahydropyrano[2,3-b]quinolin-10-ium-3,7-diol, all of which are linked to the Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1. The shared target of these compounds suggests a potential mechanism where Linderene acetate may exert its effects through a similar pathway, possibly by modulating the activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-10, which in turn influences the function of Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1. The evidence supports a plausible mechanism where Linderene acetate could act as a modulator of the receptor through a common intermediate, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-10, thereby highlighting a potential role in receptor regulation.",
